<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the TOP-LEVEL app bundle only (electron-builder's default
  path for this file — see electron-builder.config.mjs's doc comment).
  Nested binaries (Electron's own helper apps, and everything under
  Resources/server) get entitlements.mac.inherit.plist instead — see that
  file, and tasks/electron-app.md §5 Phase 5.

  Two entitlements, both because Electron's own hardened-runtime build needs
  them, not because a checklist said so:

    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
      V8 (the main process's own JS engine) marks pages executable at
      runtime. Without this, the hardened runtime's default deny blocks JIT
      and the app cannot start.
    - com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      Electron/V8 allocate executable memory that isn't itself backed by a
      code-signed page (e.g. some JIT paths, and Electron's own known need —
      see https://github.com/electron/electron/issues/27862 and the
      hardened-runtime guidance electron-builder's own docs link to).

    - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      REQUIRED. Added 2026-08-13 against the concrete failure this file's
      previous revision asked for. That revision reasoned: "our own `.node`
      modules are re-signed with Mo's identity, so there is no cross-identity
      library in the picture." The premise was true and the conclusion was
      wrong, because it counted only the libraries WE ship. It missed the
      ones the product exists to load.

      The failure. Opening the dogfood prototype in the packaged, signed,
      notarized app died with:

        failed to load config from …/ai-gateway-prototype/vite.config.ts
        Cause: Cannot find module @rollup/rollup-darwin-arm64. npm has a bug
        related to optional dependencies (npm/cli#4828)…

      That message is rollup's own, and it is a red herring — the module was
      installed and intact the whole time. Rollup's `native.js` wraps ANY
      failure to load its platform binary in that one npm-optional-deps
      story. MEASURED, calling `process.dlopen` directly on the same file
      under `ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 Desde`:

        code signature … not valid for use in process: mapping process and
        mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs

      That is macOS library validation, which the hardened runtime turns on
      by default. The `.node` is `adhoc, linker-signed` (npm publishes it
      that way; `TeamIdentifier=not set`). Our process is signed with Team ID
      JWK4LSZPKZ. Different Team IDs, so dyld refuses the mapping.

      Why the entitlement is the fix, and not a workaround. The prototype's
      native modules live in the USER'S project directory. They are npm
      artifacts we neither ship nor sign, and re-signing a customer's
      `node_modules` is not something this app gets to do. MEASURED: all
      three natives in the dogfood prototype fail identically without this
      entitlement (`@rollup/rollup-darwin-arm64`, `fsevents`,
      `@parcel/watcher-darwin-arm64`) and all three load with it. The
      in-process dev-server host — the default path for vite, nuxt,
      react-router and next — cannot work on macOS without it. There is no
      narrower entitlement: library validation is on or off.

      What it costs, stated plainly. The process will load any validly-formed
      dylib regardless of signer. We accept that because the process ALREADY
      executes arbitrary code from the user's project by design: it imports
      their `vite.config.ts` and every plugin it names. A native module is
      not a new trust boundary here, it is the same one in a different file
      format.

      Scope: this file only. entitlements.mac.inherit.plist does NOT get it —
      see that file. The process that loads project natives is the top-level
      executable, because `process.execPath` under ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE is
      `Contents/MacOS/Desde` (desktop/child.ts:121, main.ts:579), and the
      top-level bundle is exactly what this plist signs.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
